.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt IFCONFIG 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ifconfig
.Nd configure network interface parameters
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl a
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl u
.Nm
.Fl l
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl u
.Nm
.Ar interface
.Op Cm inet
.Op Ar address Ns Op / Ns Ar prefixlen
.Op Cm netmask Ar mask
.Op Cm broadcast Ar address
.Op Cm alias | Cm -alias | Cm delete
.Op Cm up | Cm down
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
shows the network interfaces and sets their IPv4 address and whether they
are up.
With no arguments, or
.Fl a ,
it describes every interface; with an
.Ar interface
alone, that one.
.Fl d
and
.Fl u
keep only the interfaces that are down, or up.
.Fl l
lists the interfaces' names on one line instead.
.Pp
Each description is a first line with the interface's name, its flags (in
hexadecimal and by name), its metric and its MTU, then a line with its
Ethernet address, if it has one, and a line with its IPv4 address, its mask
in hexadecimal and, for an interface that broadcasts, its broadcast address.
.Nm inet
alone after the interface shows only the address line.
.Pp
The flags are:
.Bl -tag -width MULTICAST -compact
.It Dv UP
The interface is up.
.It Dv BROADCAST
It can broadcast.
.It Dv LOOPBACK
It is a loopback interface.
.It Dv RUNNING
Its driver has resources allocated.
.It Dv SIMPLEX
It can't hear its own transmissions (an Ethernet).
.It Dv MULTICAST
It can do multicast.
.El
.Pp
The arguments after the interface change it, and only the superuser may
give them:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar address Ns Op / Ns Ar prefixlen
Give the interface this IPv4 address, in dotted form, replacing the one it
had.
A prefix length gives the mask; without it, or
.Cm netmask ,
the address's class decides it, as in the BSDs.
Giving an address brings the interface up, unless
.Cm down
is also given.
.It Cm netmask Ar mask
The mask, dotted
.Pq Li 255.255.255.0
or hexadecimal
.Pq Li 0xffffff00 ;
it must be contiguous.
Without an
.Ar address ,
it changes the current address's mask.
.It Cm broadcast Ar address
The broadcast address.
It is always the subnet's: this is accepted only when it names that one.
.It Cm alias
Add
.Ar address .
An OxideBSD interface has a single IPv4 address, so this works only on an
interface without one, or with that same address.
.It Cm -alias , Cm delete
Remove the address,
.Ar address
if given.
.It Cm up , Cm down
Bring the interface up, or take it down.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
The interfaces of a machine under QEMU:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ifconfig
rl0: flags=8843<UP,BROADCAST,RUNNING,SIMPLEX,MULTICAST> metric 0 mtu 1500
	ether 52:54:00:12:34:56
	inet 10.0.2.15 netmask 0xffffff00 broadcast 10.0.2.255
lo0: flags=8049<UP,LOOPBACK,RUNNING,MULTICAST> metric 0 mtu 16384
	inet 127.0.0.1 netmask 0xff000000
.Ed
.Pp
Set an address by hand, and remove it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ifconfig rl0 inet 10.0.2.15/24
# ifconfig rl0 inet 10.0.2.15 netmask 255.255.255.0
# ifconfig rl0 delete
.Ed
.Pp
At boot,
.Pa /etc/rc.d/netif
runs
.Nm
with each
.Va ifconfig_ Ns Ar interface
of
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "ifconfig: interface %s does not exist"
No interface has that name.
.It "ifconfig: ioctl (SIOCSIFADDR): Operation not permitted"
A change was asked for by a user other than the superuser.
.It "ifconfig: %s: only one address per interface (remove %s first)"
.Cm alias
was given for an interface that already has another address.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr dhclient 8 ,
.Xr route 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
command appeared in
.Bx 4.2 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
.Sh BUGS
An interface has at most one IPv4 address, and nothing else about it (MTU,
metric, Ethernet address, media) can be changed.
IPv6 isn't supported.
There are no
.Cm media
and
.Cm status
lines: the kernel doesn't know the link's state.
